<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Azarmidokht</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 631 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Azarmidokht was a Sasanian queen who ruled briefly around 630–631, during a period of rapid succession after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-ii">Khosrow II</a>. She was one of two women, with her sister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boran">Boran</a>, to occupy the Sasanian throne. Her short reign reflects the political chaos that weakened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> shortly before the Arab conquest.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/azarmidokht.jpg" alt="Azarmidokht" width="320" height="154" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Classical Numismatic Group; [1] · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AzarmidokhtCoinHistoryofIran.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Azarmidokht was a daughter of the Sasanian shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-ii">Khosrow II</a>, who was overthrown and executed on 28 February 628 by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kavad-ii">Kavad II</a>. Kavad had all his brothers and half-brothers executed, including the heir Mardanshah; Azarmidokht and her sister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boran">Boran</a> reportedly scolded him for it, and he became remorseful. The fall of Khosrow led to a civil war of four years, in which the most powerful nobles gained autonomy and the Persian (Parsig) and Parthian (Pahlav) noble families resumed their hostilities.</p><p>A plague swept the western provinces and killed half their population, Kavad among them. His young son Ardashir III was killed two years later by the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahrbaraz">Shahrbaraz</a>, who was murdered forty-three days later in a coup by the Pahlav leader Farrukh Hormizd, who helped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boran">Boran</a> take the throne. Boran was deposed and replaced by her cousin Shapur-i Shahrvaraz, who was deposed in under a year by the Parsig faction led by Piruz Khosrow, who helped Azarmidokht ascend the throne.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>On becoming queen, Azarmidokht stated that the country would be run as it had been under her father. Farrukh Hormizd asked her to marry him, to strengthen his authority and create a modus vivendi between the Pahlav and the Parsig; she declined. He then declared himself leader of the people and began to mint coins as a monarch would, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istakhr">Istakhr</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahavand">Nahavand</a>. Azarmidokht supposedly allied herself with the Mihranid Siyavakhsh, grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahram-chobin">Bahram Chobin</a>, and with his help had Farrukh Hormizd killed.</p><p>Her coins followed the style of her father's: his portrait with the winged crown of Verethragna on the obverse, with the inscription khwarrah abzūd, increase in glory, and two attendants at a fire altar on the reverse. The portrait is read as a claim to legitimacy through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-ii">Khosrow II</a>, regarded as the last rightful king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, and not as a response to her gender.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Farrukh Hormizd's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostam-farrokhzad">Rostam Farrokhzad</a>, stationed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a>, succeeded him as leader of the Pahlav. To avenge his father he marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesiphon">Ctesiphon</a>, defeating every army of Azarmidokht that he met, then defeated Siyavakhsh and captured the capital. Azarmidokht was soon afterwards blinded and killed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostam">Rostam</a>, who restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boran">Boran</a>. The centre of power possibly drifted to the northeast, the Pahlav homeland, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazdegerd-iii">Yazdegerd III</a> later fled to seek aid against the Arab invasion. Islamic accounts depict Azarmidokht as beautiful and intelligent, attribute to her the building of a castle at Asadabad, and give her the title the Just.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Azarmidokht” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A succession crisis followed in which perhaps a dozen rulers claimed the throne within four years, among them the queens Boran and <b>Azarmidokht</b>, none of whom could command the loyalty of the great noble houses or the army.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesiphon">Ctesiphon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.09,44.58&amp;year=621" aria-label="Ctesiphon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Azarmidokht"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6033 4669h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6033 4669h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=33.09,44.58&amp;year=621">What was here in 621 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
